## Step 2: Test Device Communication & Udev Setup
Before you try to flash anything, you must verify that Ubuntu can actually "see" your Tecno Spark 8 (KG6K) in both standard and fastboot modes without permission errors.
## 1. Test ADB Mode (While the phone is powered ON)

   1. On your Tecno, go to Settings > System > Developer Options and toggle USB Debugging to ON.
   2. Plug the phone into your Ubuntu laptop via USB.
   3. In your terminal, run:
   
   adb devices
   
   4. Look at your phone's screen. A popup will appear asking to "Allow USB Debugging?". Check the box for "Always allow" and tap OK.
   5. Run adb devices again. It should change from unauthorized to device.

## 2. Test Fastboot Mode (The critical flashing environment)
To reboot your Tecno into Fastboot mode directly from Ubuntu, type:

adb reboot bootloader

Your phone's screen will go dark and reboot to a black screen with tiny text at the bottom saying "FASTBOOT MODE" or "ORANGE STATE".
Once it is in that state, type:

fastboot devices


* If it shows a string of numbers followed by "fastboot": You are perfectly connected and ready to proceed.
* If it shows blank or "no permissions": Linux needs a security rule (udev rule) to talk to MediaTek chips. If you get a permission error, run this command to fix it, replug the phone, and try again:

sudo apt install android-sdk-platform-tools-common


